items: conformance-guard-enforced
paths: tests/conformance/run.sh
scope: |
  Sergiy: a run died of memory exhaustion. Diagnosed, and the cause is mine.

  `scripts/conformance` exists precisely for this and its own header states the failure mode:
  run.sc spawns a subprocess PER CASE x 3 lanes, the JVM lane is a cold scala-cli compile each time,
  and bare in ~15 parallel worktrees with uncapped forks (no -Xmx => ~1/4-RAM default, ~9 GB ceiling
  each) the AGGREGATE saturates host RAM. AGENTS.md:101 names it the RAM-bounded entrypoint.

  I called `tests/conformance/run.sh --no-memo` DIRECTLY, three times over the full corpus, while
  siblings were building in their own worktrees. Bare run.sh takes no semaphore and caps no heap.
  Measured aftermath: 139,831 pageouts and 526 MB swap in use, with the guard never firing (it sheds
  BUILD JVMs, not conformance forks) and no jetsam kill — so nothing reported it; the machine just
  swapped.

  Fix, and it is the same argument I made for [skip ci] earlier today: a rule that depends on every
  agent remembering it is a wish, not a rule. `tests/conformance/run.sh` re-execs itself through
  `scripts/conformance` unless already inside it (a marker env var the wrapper exports), so the bare
  path becomes the guarded path. Additive: the wrapper already forwards all args, and an explicit
  opt-out stays available for anyone who genuinely wants it unbounded.

  NOT changing: `scripts/conformance` itself, the semaphore design, or any runner behaviour.
next: add the marker export to the wrapper, re-exec in run.sh, verify both paths and the opt-out
